## Per-domain MCP policy: configurable tool set and ontology context

Context: the MCP surface was uniform — every MCP-enabled domain published all
11 tools and every ontology attachment, whatever its shape. A domain with no
Unity Catalog functions still advertised `invoke_entity_action` (prompt budget
spent on a wrong-tool branch), and a domain whose value is its cross-domain
bridges had no way to say so, since the bridge hint read like every other
hint. Each domain now decides, from a new MCP tab in Domain / Information,
which tools it publishes and how its datasets, bridges and actions are
surfaced. An unconfigured domain reproduces the pre-0.8 behaviour exactly: the
policy defaults to the empty blob, disabled tools are stored rather than
enabled ones, and a missing context key reads as `normal` — so no backfill.

Falls under the AI feature lifecycle gate (`.cursor/12`): SPEC.md and an eval
dataset ship with the change.

Changes:

1. `src/back/core/mcp_tools.py` (new)
   Single source of truth for the configurable surface: `MCP_REGISTRY_TOOLS`
   (the 4 always-on tools), `MCP_DOMAIN_TOOLS` (the 7 configurable ones),
   `MCP_CONTEXT_FEATURES`, `MCP_CONTEXT_MODES`, and `coerce_mcp_policy`, which
   drops unknown tools, registry tools, unknown features and bad modes rather
   than raising, so a hand-edited registry row cannot break domain loading.
2. `src/back/objects/registry/store/lakebase/schema.sql`
   Added `domains.mcp_policy jsonb NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'::jsonb`.
3. `src/back/objects/registry/store/lakebase/store.py`
   Added `_ensure_domains_mcp_policy_column` lazy self-heal (same
   ownership-aware pattern as `review_quorum`), wired at `initialize` plus the
   three runtime paths, and promoted the column through the `domains` UPSERT,
   `read_version` and `list_domains_with_metadata`.
4. `src/back/objects/registry/store/base.py`
