================================================================================
Fix — MCP tab: query_graphql stayed clickable on a read-only domain
================================================================================

Context: on a read-only domain the MCP tab correctly froze every control except
one — the `Query GraphQL` checkbox stayed interactive, letting a viewer (or a
reader on a PUBLISHED / IN-REVIEW version, or an editor locked out of a DRAFT)
toggle the domain's published tool set.

Root cause: `permissions.css` neutralises form fields with a generic rule that
exempts search / filter / query inputs, including `:not([id*="query"])` — an
exemption meant for query editors. The tool checkboxes are rendered as
`#mcpTool_<tool>`, so the tool literally named `query_graphql` produced
`#mcpTool_query_graphql`, matched the exemption, and escaped the lockdown. Only
that one control was affected, which is why the tab looked correct otherwise.
The same trap was waiting for any future tool whose name contains "search" or
"filter".

Changes:

1. `src/front/static/global/css/permissions.css` — added a read-only rule
   gating `.js-mcp-tool`, `.js-mcp-context` and `#mcpToolsSelectAll` by class.
   The lockdown no longer depends on what a tool happens to be called. Gated
   for all three read-only signals (`read-only-version`, `role-viewer`,
   `read-only-locked`): the policy is a property of the domain, so editing it
   is a domain write. Server-side enforcement was already correct — this is the
   UI catching up.

2. `tests/units/front/test_view_mode_write_gates.py` — two regression tests:
   the three MCP selectors sit in a read-only disable rule, and no MCP rule
   leans on an id derived from a tool name. Mutation-checked: removing the CSS
   rule fails the suite.

